I"m<h2 id="the-question">The question</h2>

<p>Most MRI segmentation models learn from outlines drawn by hand. That is tedious, and more so when several structures must be outlined on every scan.</p>

<h2 id="what-we-did">What we did</h2>

<p>Spine-GFlow combines what a neural network learns from the image with anatomical prior knowledge. A rule-based method first produces rough seed areas automatically, a proposal step blends multi-scale image features with anatomy, and a training loss refines pixel classification. No manual annotation is used. We tested it on two independent datasets, one with images from three different scanners.</p>

<h2 id="what-we-found">What we found</h2>

<p>Without any manual labels, segmentation of vertebral bodies, discs and the spinal canal was comparable to a fully supervised model (mean Dice score, a measure of overlap with the expert outline where 1 is perfect: 0.914 vs 0.916), and it held up across scanner settings.</p>

<h2 id="why-it-matters">Why it matters</h2>

<p>Removing the annotation bottleneck makes it far easier to build spine-imaging tools for new hospitals and scanners.</p>
